    "slot_value": "Coordinate software updates centrally across all branches so changes can be applied consistently while normal library service continues. If a change only affects one branch's own staff, workflow, or local settings and does not touch shared services, it can proceed independently. If it affects shared services that other branches rely on, such as a shared catalog rule, circulation policy, user privilege, or an interface, it needs coordination or a wider change. Updates limited to a branch-specific workstation or something that doesn’t connect to shared services can usually be applied locally. If an update touches the core ILS, shared databases, authentication, interfaces, or anything that could affect more than one branch, it should be scheduled centrally so timing and rollback are controlled. If the issue is clearly cross-branch or tied to a shared service, central should take ownership right away because one branch can’t really coordinate that on its own. If it’s local but has ripple effects, the branch can lead initially, but central should be pulled in quickly to coordinate and avoid conflicting actions. The practical rule is whoever has the best visibility and authority over the impacted service should own the response, while the other side stays informed and supports. If response speed matters, there should be a quick handoff path so ownership doesn’t get stuck in debate. If the action only affects one branch’s local environment, it can stay local as long as it doesn’t impact shared services or other branches. If it touches a shared server, central database, common interface, or anything that could cascade to multiple branches, then it should be treated as system-wide and handled centrally. The practical line is whether the change has isolated impact or shared impact. If there’s any real chance it could affect more than one branch, central review or control is required before it goes ahead. A branch can keep ownership if the issue is clearly r