    "slot_value": "Privileges should be role-based and limited to people who actually need them for their job, with stronger restrictions on anything that can be changed versus just viewed. Sensitive records like audit logs, account details, and backup information should be visible to administrators and support staff only when they are handling a legitimate operational issue, with write access tightly controlled. If the data includes patron or security-related information, it should be masked or partially displayed unless someone has an approved reason to see the full record. Access for troubleshooting by someone outside the normal admin role should be temporary, logged, and escalated through the appropriate support process. Access should be limited to administrators and support staff who are actively responsible for monitoring or resolving the issue, and only for the systems they need to touch; if a record contains security-sensitive details, it should be even more restricted, with audit logging on every view or change. Records tied to an external interface or a security issue should be treated as highly sensitive by default, with access limited to the smallest support group that can work the issue. If they need to be shared wider for investigation, that should be temporary and deliberate, not the normal state. Administrators should be able to inspect status, review logs, restart services, clear stuck jobs, and verify connectivity or configuration when needed; disabling or re-enabling an interface may be allowed but should be controlled because it can affect branch operations. Anything that changes data or has a broad service impact should be more tightly restricted and probably require additional approval or at least a clear audit trail. Anything that can interrupt branch operations should be limited to authorized administrators with a clear operational need, ideally done during a low-impact window if timing matters, and disabling or re-enabling an interface should have explicit approval or documented authorization.",
